\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Proposition}
\title{The algebraic exchange criterion}
\date{2026-11-26}

\p{Let #{V=V_+\oplus V_-} be a direct sum of modules over a
unital ring, with a group #{G} acting by module automorphisms
and preserving the summands. A linear
\vocabk{equivariant sector exchange}{fdip-018A} exists if and
only if #{V_+} and #{V_-} are isomorphic as #{G}-modules.
When it exists one can choose its square to be the identity.}

\proof{
\p{Restrict an exchange to #{V_+}. This gives a bijection onto
#{V_-} that commutes with the action and respects the scalars.
Conversely, given such an isomorphism #{b:V_+\to V_-}, its
inverse also intertwines the actions. The map
#{u(v_+,v_-)=(b^{-1}v_-,bv_+)} therefore commutes with the
group, interchanges the summands and squares to the identity.}

\p{If the summands are given by a grading #{\Gamma}, then
over a ring in which #{2} is invertible they are the images of
the complementary projections #{(\bbI\pm\Gamma)/2}.
For a supplied direct sum the restriction and assembly use
no division.}
}
